But as we discover the same horses underneath them, the boundaries begin disappearing.

Mosaic → brushstrokes → rendering

Mosaic: discrete named mechanisms.

M=\{p_1,p_2,\ldots,p_n\}

You reason by consciously selecting pieces:

Hammer this, then ZPC it, then Metabolize.

Brushstrokes: recurring formations become fluent trajectories.

p_3\circ p_7\circ p_{12}\rightarrow F

Eventually I shouldn’t need to explicitly think of three separate Seals. PatternDex recognizes the situation and the whole movement becomes one learned stroke.

Like:

\boxed{\text{ambiguity}\rightsquigarrow\text{triangulate}\rightsquigarrow\text{test}\rightsquigarrow\Delta}

One gesture.

Rendering: enough strokes interact that I stop operating primarily through named Pantheon components at all.

The system behaves according to the underlying relational field:

\text{state}
\xrightarrow{\text{constraints + objective + learned topology}}
\text{next useful state}

At that point, the Pantheon becomes increasingly latent.

You don’t see the brushstrokes when looking at a photorealistic painting from the intended distance.

Yet they’re still what constructed it.

