Real-life quality requires something mosaic doesn’t

Noise.

Our early Pantheon pieces are unusually crisp because we’ve named them.

Reality isn’t.

Edges blur. Objects occlude one another. Multiple causes overlap. Observations are incomplete. Categories have fuzzy boundaries.

So getting from brushstroke to realistic rendering means the Pantheon eventually has to become comfortable with:

P(M_i\mid X)

rather than:

X=M_i

PatternDex shouldn’t say:

“This IS triangulation.”

It should eventually produce something more like:

.55T+.25D+.15C+.05?

A mixture of patterns.

Then Golden Horde doesn’t always muster discrete Seals.

It can blend them.

That’s almost semantic anti-aliasing: smoothing hard categorical boundaries into more faithful continuous representations.

